+## The language preference
+
+One setting, coupled: the interface locale and the model's reply language are
+the same preference. The worker stores it as `response_language` (ISO 639-1,
+`pt`), the BFF proxies it at `GET`/`PUT /api/preferences`
+(`src/app/api/preferences/route.ts`), and the client maps in both directions
+with `normalizeLocale()` (`pt` → `pt-BR`) and `toResponseLanguage()`
+(`pt-BR` → `pt`). The user chooses; nothing else writes the preference (the
+worker's per-turn language detection is telemetry only).
+
+**One owner.** `LocalePreferenceProvider`
+(`src/components/providers/locale-preference-provider.tsx`, mounted once by
+`AssistantProvider`, i.e. in the authenticated area only) is the only code
+that talks to the route and the only caller of `setLocale` for it. It exposes
+`useLocalePreference()` → `{ locale, ready, choose(locale) }`; the JSDoc
+there is the spec. Two consequences are deliberate:
+
+- A slow mount-time load can never revert the user's pick: `choose`
+  supersedes any load still in flight (the pending `GET` is aborted and its
+  result ignored).
+- The chrome never flips under an animating reply: `AssistantProvider`
+  passes `hold={isLoading}`, so a value the load delivers mid-stream is
+  held and applied when the reply lands, and the picker in the user menu
+  (`src/components/user-menu.tsx`) is disabled while a reply is in flight
+  with the hint `userMenu.languageLockedWhileReplying`. Sending is never
+  blocked on the preference load (the worker replies in the persisted
+  language anyway).
+- The first-visit seed is the browser's locale (`getClientLocale()`, read
+  at PUT time), never React state: during hydration the first render still
+  holds the server snapshot, and seeding that would pin `en` for a pt-BR
+  browser forever.
+
+Display names in the picker are not translation targets: each language is
+named in itself, and the names live in the registry, not in JSX, so the lint
+guard needs no allow-list entry for them.
 
